5. Secondly, before the expiry of 90 days, the civil Court went into annual vacation and it was closed for 30 days. So they would get the benefit of 30 days in counting the limitation. Thus, the trial Court allowed the application for substitution upon the aforesaid two grounds implacably condoning the delay of one day.&nbsp; 

 6. Article 176 of the Limitation Act provides that within 90 days from the date of the death of the sole plaintiff or appellant, the heirs are to be substituted, from the date of the death of the deceased. The substitution is to be made under Order 22 of the Code of Civil Procedure. In case of non-substitution within 90 days under Order 22 rule 1 of the Code of Civil Procedure an application for substitution can be made under Order 22 rule 3(1) within 60 days after expiry of 90 days.&nbsp; 

 10. Mr Khaled, the learned Counsel appearing for the defendants opposite parties, on the other hand, submits that the application for substitution was within limitation and is not delayed. According to him, section 9 of the General Clauses Act will come into play from the next date of the death of the sole plaintiff. So, according to him the limitation for abatement started and thus from 12-10-95 just on the day i.e. date on of which the abatement started, the application of the plaintiff for substitution cannot be said to be delayed and it was rather within time.&nbsp;
